---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Figure1 = `[42, 17, 93, 8, 55, 31]

Look at everything. The smallest is 8.
Put it at the front, and move 42 to where 8 was.

[8, 17, 93, 42, 55, 31]
 ^^ done. Never look at it again.

Now do the same to what is left.`;

const Figure2 = `let smallest = i;

for (let j = i + 1; j < list.length; j++) {
  if (list[j] < list[smallest]) {
    smallest = j;
  }
}`;

const Figure3 = `let temp = list[i];
list[i] = list[smallest];
list[smallest] = temp;`;

const Figure4 = `list[i] = list[smallest];
list[smallest] = list[i];   // both are now the same value`;

const Task1 = `let scores = [42, 17, 93, 8, 55, 31];

function selectionSort(list) {
  let comparisons = 0;

  for (let i = 0; i < list.length - 1; i++) {
    let smallest = i;

    for (let j = i + 1; j < list.length; j++) {
      comparisons = comparisons + 1;

      if (list[j] < list[smallest]) {
        smallest = j;
      }
    }

    let temp = list[i];
    list[i] = list[smallest];
    list[smallest] = temp;

    console.log("After pass " + (i + 1) + ": " + list);
  }

  console.log("Comparisons: " + comparisons);
  return list;
}

selectionSort(scores);
`;

const Task2 = `// Add this below the first call.

console.log("");
console.log("--- a list that is already sorted ---");
selectionSort([8, 17, 31, 42, 55, 93]);
`;

const Task3 = `let names = ["Ines", "Ravi", "Nadia", "Owen", "Priya"];
let scores = [72, 95, 88, 61, 79];

// Sort these into a leaderboard: highest score first.
//
// The two lists have to stay lined up. If Ravi's 95 moves to the
//   front, "Ravi" has to move with it.
//
// Print the finished leaderboard as numbered lines.
//
// Then answer in a comment: what breaks if you sort scores
//   and forget to move names?
`;

const ExampleOutput = `After pass 1: 8,17,93,42,55,31
After pass 2: 8,17,93,42,55,31
After pass 3: 8,17,31,42,55,93
After pass 4: 8,17,31,42,55,93
After pass 5: 8,17,31,42,55,93
Comparisons: 15`;

const NotationJs = `let temp = list[i];
list[i] = list[smallest];
list[smallest] = temp;`;

const NotationPseudo = `temp ← aList[i]
aList[i] ← aList[smallest]
aList[smallest] ← temp`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 2.12: Sorting and Comparing Algorithms</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 2.12</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Sorting and Comparing Algorithms</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>Selection Sort</p>
              <p>Swapping Without Losing a Value</p>
              <p>Work That Does Not Depend on Luck</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Why Bother Sorting</h2>
              <p>Last session: binary search needs a sorted list.</p>
              <p class="fragment">So somebody has to sort it.</p>
              <p class="fragment">That somebody is you, today.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>How You Would Do It by Hand</h2>
              <Code code={Figure1} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Selection Sort" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">Find the smallest. Move it to the front. Repeat on the rest.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Finding the Smallest</h2>
              <Code code={Figure2} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="The Inner Loop" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">This is Algorithm A from 2.9, running on part of the list.</p>
              <p class="fragment">It tracks the <b>index</b>, not the value. You need to know where it was.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>A Loop Inside a Loop</h2>
              <p>Outer loop: which position are we filling?</p>
              <p class="fragment">Inner loop: what is the smallest thing left?</p>
              <p class="fragment">You met this shape at 1.21. Now it is doing something.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Swap</h2>
              <Code code={Figure3} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="Three Lines, Not Two" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">Why three?</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Because Two Loses One</h2>
              <Code code={Figure4} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="The Broken Version" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">The first line overwrites the value the second line needs.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><code>temp</code> exists to hold it for one line.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Watch What It Does Not Know</h2>
              <p>Some passes change nothing at all.</p>
              <p class="fragment">The list can be finished with three passes left to run.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>Selection sort has no way to notice.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Which Means</h2>
              <p>An already-sorted list costs exactly the same as a scrambled one.</p>
              <p class="fragment">15 comparisons either way, on six items.</p>
              <p class="fragment">No best case. That is unusual, and it is worth knowing.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Describing selection sort as repeatedly finding the smallest</li>
                <li>Explaining why a swap needs three assignments</li>
                <li>Counting the comparisons a sort performs</li>
                <li>Showing that selection sort does the same work on any input</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Selection sort</dt>
                  <dd>Repeatedly select the smallest remaining item and place it next.</dd>
                  <dt>Swap</dt>
                  <dd>Exchange two values, using a third variable to avoid losing one.</dd>
                  <dt>Pass</dt>
                  <dd>One full trip of the outer loop.</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <p>
            Create a new <b>JavaScript program</b> named{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">2-12-sort</b>.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: Sort It</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Write the code below and run it.</li>
            <li>
              <b>Two of the five passes change nothing.</b> Find them, and say in
              a comment why the list did not move on those passes.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <Code code={Task1} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="Selection Sort" class="mt-3" />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: Sort Something Already Sorted</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Add the extra call and run it again.</li>
            <li>
              <b>Compare the two comparison counts.</b> Record both in a comment.
            </li>
            <li>
              Then answer: linear search got faster when it got lucky. Why does
              this sort not?
            </li>
          </ul>
          <Code code={Task2} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="Selection Sort" class="mt-3" />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: A Leaderboard</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Fill in the comment block.</li>
          </ul>
          <Code code={Task3} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="Selection Sort" class="mt-3" />

          <h5 class="mt-4">Challenge (optional)</h5>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Add a check that skips the swap when <code>smallest</code> is
              already <code>i</code>. Count how many swaps that saves on the Task 1
              list. Then explain why the <b>comparison</b> count did not budge.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Exam Notation -->
        <section id="exam-notation">
          <h2>Same Idea, Exam Notation</h2>

          <div class="row g-3 my-4">
            <div class="col-md-6">
              <Code
                code={NotationJs}
                frame="code"
                lang="javascript"
                title="JavaScript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-6">
              <Code
                code={NotationPseudo}
                frame="code"
                lang="txt"
                title="Exam Notation"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The swap is one of the few places where the two notations line up
            almost exactly. <code>aList[i] ← aList[j]</code> is written on the
            reference sheet as its own list operation, so the exam expects you to
            read it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>One difference that matters more here than usual.</b> On the
            reference sheet, <code>aList ← bList</code> assigns a{" "}
            <b>copy</b> of the list. In JavaScript it does not &mdash; it makes a
            second name for the same list, and sorting through one name changes
            what you see through the other.
          </p>

          <p>
            So a sort question in exam notation can hand you two list names and
            expect the original to be untouched. <b>The same question in
            JavaScript would have a different answer.</b> This is the single
            biggest mismatch between the two notations in the whole course.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>Task 1 should produce exactly this.</p>

          <Code
            code={ExampleOutput}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            title="Example Output"
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            Your leaderboard in Task 3 will look different, but Ravi must be
            first and Owen must be last.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer these before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>Sorting six items took 15 comparisons. Binary search on six items takes about three. When is it worth paying the 15?</li>
            <li>Selection sort cannot tell that it has finished early. Describe, in words, what you would have to add so it could.</li>
            <li>In Task 3 the names had to move with the scores. What does that tell you about the risk of keeping related data in two separate lists?</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit the required files to the appropriate dropbox.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
